<BaseLayout
	title="Start here"
	description="Yeh Bro’s blueprint for getting into the best shape of your life — mindset, training, food, sleep, supplements that matter, then optional deeper culture reading."
>
	<article class="prose-wrap journey">
		<p class="eyebrow">Blueprint</p>
		<h1>Start here</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			Here’s how I’d get someone into the best shape of their life — not the flashiest eight-week
			transformation, the one that still holds in twelve months. Six steps. Do them in order. Skip
			the deep culture / PED stuff until the basics feel boring in a good way.
		</p>

		<nav class="step-nav" aria-label="Journey steps">
			<a href="#step-1">1 · Mindset</a>
			<a href="#step-2">2 · Training</a>
			<a href="#step-3">3 · Food</a>
			<a href="#step-4">4 · Sleep</a>
			<a href="#step-5">5 · Supplements</a>
			<a href="#step-6">6 · Optional deeper</a>
		</nav>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-1">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 1</p>
			<h2>Mindset — honesty about where you’re starting</h2>
			<p>
				Be straight with yourself: training age, sleep, stress, how consistent you’ve actually been.
				Eight-week “before/after” feeds lie with lighting, pumps, filters, and sometimes pharmacology.
				Your job is a repeatable week you can run when life is average — not a hero week you abandon.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Pick one goal for 12 weeks</strong> — get stronger, lose fat, or rebuild the habit. Not all three at once.</li>
				<li><strong>Measure something boring</strong> — sessions completed, body weight trend, a few lifts. Screenshots of peak lighting don’t count.</li>
				<li><strong>Ignore “natty or not” bait</strong> until your own process is solid. Comparison without context is how people quit.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Best shape of your life is a process score, not a peak still.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-2">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 2</p>
			<h2>Training — consistency beats complexity</h2>
			<p>
				If I were programming for most people: lift <strong>3–4 days a week</strong>, hit the big
				patterns (squat/hinge, push, pull, carry or core), and add load or reps over time. That’s
				progressive overload. Fancy splits can wait.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Simple split that works:</strong> Full body 3×/week, or Upper / Lower 4×/week.
					Keep the same lifts long enough to get good at them.
					<a href="/blog/beginner-full-body-program">Full-body template</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/how-many-days-to-lift">How many days</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Leave 1–3 reps in the tank</strong> most sets. Destroying yourself every session is
					ego, not a plan. Progress the logbook:
					<a href="/blog/progressive-overload">Progressive overload</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Cardio:</strong> walk most days
					(<a href="/blog/walking-for-lifters">walking guide</a>). Add one harder conditioning
					session if recovery allows — not daily redline. Fried? 
					<a href="/blog/deload-week">Deload</a>.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				When you’re ready for phased cut → health pivot → lean bulk education:
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint">Bodybuilding blueprint</a>.
			</p>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Show up, progress the logbook, recover. Program-hopping is procrastination.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-3">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 3</p>
			<h2>Food — protein first, phases when you’re ready</h2>
			<p>
				Don’t obsess over apps before you can hit protein and roughly the right calories most days.
				Here’s the order I’d use:
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<strong>Protein:</strong> about <strong>1.6–2.2 g per kg</strong> body weight as a practical
					range for lifters.
					<a href="/blog/protein-targets-for-lifters">Protein targets</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/whey-vs-food-protein">Whey vs food</a>
				</li>
				<li><strong>Maintenance first:</strong> eat roughly enough to hold weight while training quality rises.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Deficit when ready:</strong> ~15–20% below maintenance to lose fat without torching
					lifts — <a href="/blog/cutting-without-wrecking-lifts">cutting guide</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Surplus when ready:</strong> a small bump (~10%) to grow — not a dirty bulk that
					undoes the cut — <a href="/blog/lean-bulk-basics">lean bulk basics</a>.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				Phased food + training detail lives in the
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint-phase-1-cut">cut</a>,
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint-phase-2-health-pivot">health pivot</a>, and
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint-phase-3-lean-bulk">lean bulk</a> guides — after steps 1–4 feel normal.
			</p>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Hit protein. Adjust calories slowly. Perfect macros can wait.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-4">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 4</p>
			<h2>Sleep / recovery — the unpaid coach</h2>
			<p>
				You can out-train a bad night once. You can’t out-train a bad month. Aim for a sleep window
				you can protect most nights — same bedtime beats heroic weekend catch-up.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Treat <strong>7–9 hours</strong> as training support, not optional lifestyle fluff —
					<a href="/blog/sleep-for-lifters">sleep for lifters</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					If resting heart rate or mood is wrecked for days, <strong>deload</strong> — cut volume
					~30% for a week instead of adding more caffeine —
					<a href="/blog/deload-week">deload week</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					Stress and steps matter.
					<a href="/blog/stress-and-lifting">Stress and lifting</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/walking-for-lifters">Walking for lifters</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					Persistent fatigue, hard cuts, or aging curiosity →
					<a href="/blog/bloodwork-for-lifters">bloodwork for lifters</a>
					(labs with a clinician — not Discord diagnosis).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Recovery is part of the program. Skip it and the logbook stalls.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-5">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 5</p>
			<h2>Supplements that actually matter</h2>
			<p>
				Most tubs are noise. For average lifters building the best shape of their life, the short
				list is boring on purpose:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Creatine monohydrate</strong> — 3–5 g daily. Loading optional. Form theatre is mostly marketing.
					<a href="/blog/best-creatine-australia">Creatine guide</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/creatine-myths">Myths</a> ·
					<a href="/go/creatine-true" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">/go/creatine-true</a> ·
					<a href="/go/creatine-bulk" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">/go/creatine-bulk</a>
					· convenience backup
					<a href="/go/creatine-amz" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">/go/creatine-amz</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Protein powder</strong> — only if food is awkward. Judge cost per ~25 g protein, not flavour ads.
					<a href="/blog/best-protein-powder-australia">Protein guide</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/whey-vs-food-protein">Whey vs food</a> ·
					<a href="/go/protein-true" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">/go/protein-true</a> ·
					<a href="/go/protein-bulk" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">/go/protein-bulk</a>
					· Amazon browse
					<a href="/go/protein-amz" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">/go/protein-amz</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Caffeine</strong> — useful for training focus if you tolerate it. Not a personality.
					<a href="/blog/caffeine-dose-training">Caffeine timing</a>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Order of operations and what to skip:
				<a href="/blog/beginner-supplement-stack-australia">Beginner stack</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/supplement-label-red-flags-australia">Label red flags</a>.
			</p>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Creatine + protein convenience cover most of the useful powder lane. Everything else earns its place later.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-6">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 6 · Optional</p>
			<h2>Deeper culture — when the basics are locked</h2>
			<p>
				Gym culture has a pharmacology shadow. Ignoring it creates naivety; leading with it creates
				chaos for beginners. When you’re training consistently and want literacy — risks, bloodwork,
				legality, how forums talk about phases — go here. Not day one.
			</p>
			<div class="path-links">
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-culture-education">Bodybuilding culture</a>
				<a href="/blog/natty-or-not-physique-standards">Natty or not</a>
				<a href="/blog/bloodwork-for-lifters">Bloodwork for lifters</a>
				<a href="/blog/what-i-run-beside-testosterone">What I run beside testosterone</a>
				<a href="/blog/ped-harm-reduction-australia">PED harm reduction</a>
				<a href="/blog/ped-bloodwork-literacy">PED bloodwork literacy</a>
				<a href="/blog/ped-coming-off-under-care">Coming off under care</a>
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint">Phased blueprint</a>
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint-safety">Safety companion</a>
				<a href="/about">About Yeh Bro</a>
			</div>
			<p class="takeaway">
				<strong>Takeaway:</strong> Deeper reading is literacy, not a shopping list. Bloodwork and
				medical decisions stay with a clinician. We don’t source gear.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block close-block">
			<p class="eyebrow">Do this week</p>
			<h2>Your first seven days</h2>
			<ol>
				<li>Book 3–4 lift sessions on the calendar (Upper/Lower or full body).</li>
				<li>Hit a protein target most days — food first, powder if needed.</li>
				<li>Protect one sleep window like it’s a session.</li>
				<li>Start creatine if you want the easy win (3–5 g/day).</li>
				<li>Come back to the guides when a question is real — not when you’re doomscrolling.</li>
			</ol>
			<div class="cta-row">
				<a class="btn btn-primary" href="/blog/beginner-full-body-program">Full-body template</a>
				<a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/blog/beginner-supplement-stack-australia">Beginner stack</a>
				<a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/blog">All guides</a>
			</div>
		</section>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

